WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.492
Pitch questions often feel harder than they really are because several sound ideas arrive at once.

2
00:00:05.492 --> 00:00:10.984
Frequency decides pitch.

3
00:00:10.984 --> 00:00:16.476
Loudness is a separate sound-strength question.

4
00:00:16.476 --> 00:00:21.968
And if the sound stays in the same medium, the wave speed is already fixed well enough that a higher frequency shows up mainly as a shorter wavelength instead of as a faster sound.

5
00:00:21.968 --> 00:00:27.460
The safest opening move for this lesson is to separate those three roles before touching the numbers.

6
00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:32.952
Keep this takeaway in view: Lesson 3 becomes stable once pitch, wavelength, and loudness are kept in different conceptual boxes.

7
00:00:32.952 --> 00:00:38.444
Pitch means frequency-owned.

8
00:00:38.444 --> 00:00:43.936
A higher frequency means a higher pitch.

9
00:00:43.936 --> 00:00:49.428
Same medium means speed stays medium-owned.

10
00:00:49.428 --> 00:00:54.920
When the air stays the same, changing frequency changes wavelength more directly than speed.

11
00:00:54.920 --> 00:01:02.129
This diagram matters because it keeps three sound ideas linked in the right order.

12
00:01:02.129 --> 00:01:09.338
Frequency sets the pitch.

13
00:01:09.338 --> 00:01:16.546
In the same air, wave speed stays tied mainly to the medium, so a higher frequency means the wave cycles are packed closer together and the wavelength is shorter.

14
00:01:16.546 --> 00:01:23.755
Loudness is visible on a separate track, which prevents the learner from using amplitude language to explain pitch changes.

15
00:01:23.755 --> 00:01:30.964
Once those layers stay separate, the calculation scene later feels much cleaner.

16
00:01:30.964 --> 00:01:38.172
Keep this takeaway in view: A strong pitch explanation keeps tone height, wavelength spacing, and loudness from merging into one label.

17
00:01:38.172 --> 00:01:45.381
Frequency track: Higher cycle rate means a higher-pitched sound.

18
00:01:45.381 --> 00:01:52.590
Wavelength track: In the same medium, higher frequency gives a shorter wavelength.

19
00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:57.376
A beacon analogy helps because it gives two independent controls.

20
00:01:57.376 --> 00:02:02.162
One knob changes how quickly the flashes arrive.

21
00:02:02.162 --> 00:02:06.948
Another changes how bright each flash appears.

22
00:02:06.948 --> 00:02:11.733
If you turn up the brightness, you did not increase the flash rate.

23
00:02:11.733 --> 00:02:16.519
Sound works the same way for this lesson.

24
00:02:16.519 --> 00:02:21.305
A larger amplitude can make a sound louder, but it does not automatically make the sound higher in pitch.

25
00:02:21.305 --> 00:02:26.091
The rate idea and the strength idea must stay separate if the learner wants to answer confidently.

26
00:02:26.091 --> 00:02:30.877
Keep this takeaway in view: The analogy makes it easier to defend why louder does not automatically mean higher pitch.

27
00:02:30.877 --> 00:02:35.663
Rate knob means frequency.

28
00:02:35.663 --> 00:02:40.448
This is the control that matches pitch.

29
00:02:40.448 --> 00:02:45.234
Brightness knob means loudness analogy.

30
00:02:45.234 --> 00:02:50.020
This is a separate strength control and should not be used to answer a pitch question.

31
00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:56.712
The key relation here is wave speed equals frequency times wavelength.

32
00:02:56.712 --> 00:03:03.404
In the same air, the sound speed is treated as medium-owned, so wavelength equals speed divided by frequency.

33
00:03:03.404 --> 00:03:10.095
That means the higher-frequency tone has the shorter wavelength.

34
00:03:10.095 --> 00:03:16.787
The pitch decision comes from the frequency directly, not from the loudness and not from a guessed speed change.

35
00:03:16.787 --> 00:03:23.479
This is the exact structure the learner needs before comparing one hundred seventy hertz with six hundred eighty hertz in the worked example.

36
00:03:23.479 --> 00:03:30.171
Keep this takeaway in view: The formula scene becomes powerful once the learner knows which quantity is answering which question.

37
00:03:30.171 --> 00:03:36.863
Direct pitch clue: Compare the frequencies first before doing anything else.

38
00:03:36.863 --> 00:03:43.555
Direct wavelength clue: Use lambda = v / f with the same-medium sound speed to compare spacing between cycles.

39
00:03:43.555 --> 00:03:50.246
v = f lambda.

40
00:03:50.246 --> 00:03:56.938
in the same medium, lambda = v / f so higher f means shorter lambda.

41
00:03:56.938 --> 00:04:03.630
Use the medium to own the speed, the frequency to own the pitch, and the formula to read the wavelength.

42
00:04:03.630 --> 00:04:09.460
A strong routine for this lesson has three steps.

43
00:04:09.460 --> 00:04:15.290
First check whether the medium is the same for both sounds.

44
00:04:15.290 --> 00:04:21.120
Second compare the frequencies to answer the pitch question immediately.

45
00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:26.950
Third use the fixed medium speed and lambda equals speed divided by frequency to compare wavelengths.

46
00:04:26.950 --> 00:04:32.780
This sequence makes the reasoning clean and prevents the learner from wandering into loudness or amplitude language when the question is really about pitch and wavelength.

47
00:04:32.780 --> 00:04:38.610
Keep this takeaway in view: Medium first, frequency second, wavelength third is the safest comparison routine for Lesson 3.

48
00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:44.440
Step 1 means check the medium.

49
00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:50.270
If the sounds travel in the same air, the wave-speed story stays medium-owned.

50
00:04:50.270 --> 00:04:56.100
Step 2 means compare frequency.

51
00:04:56.100 --> 00:05:01.930
The higher frequency gives the higher pitch.

52
00:05:01.930 --> 00:05:06.753
A common weak sentence says a sound is higher pitch because it is louder.

53
00:05:06.753 --> 00:05:11.575
This is exactly the misconception that needs extra airtime.

54
00:05:11.575 --> 00:05:16.398
Loudness is linked to sound strength or amplitude.

55
00:05:16.398 --> 00:05:21.221
Pitch is linked to frequency.

56
00:05:21.221 --> 00:05:26.044
A louder sound can have exactly the same frequency and exactly the same pitch as a quieter sound.

57
00:05:26.044 --> 00:05:30.866
The safest defense is to say the quantity names out loud before comparing them.

58
00:05:30.866 --> 00:05:35.689
Keep this takeaway in view: Lesson 3 stays calm once the learner refuses to let loudness answer a pitch question.

59
00:05:35.689 --> 00:05:40.512
Weak claim means louder means higher.

60
00:05:40.512 --> 00:05:45.335
This mixes amplitude language into a frequency question.

61
00:05:45.335 --> 00:05:50.157
Correction means pitch means frequency.

62
00:05:50.157 --> 00:05:54.980
Use the cycle rate to answer tone-height questions.

63
00:05:54.980 --> 00:05:59.791
Run four checks.

64
00:05:59.791 --> 00:06:04.602
Pitch follows frequency.

65
00:06:04.602 --> 00:06:09.413
In the same medium, higher frequency means shorter wavelength.

66
00:06:09.413 --> 00:06:14.224
Sound speed in the same air is treated as medium-owned rather than frequency-owned.

67
00:06:14.224 --> 00:06:19.035
And loudness belongs to amplitude or sound strength, not to pitch.

68
00:06:19.035 --> 00:06:23.845
Those four checks capture the whole lesson structure before the numbers appear.

69
00:06:23.845 --> 00:06:28.656
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready once the learner can explain why higher pitch in the same air means shorter wavelength, not faster sound.

70
00:06:28.656 --> 00:06:33.467
Pitch means higher f -> higher pitch.

71
00:06:33.467 --> 00:06:38.278
Compare frequencies first when tone height is being discussed.

72
00:06:38.278 --> 00:06:43.089
Wavelength means higher f -> shorter lambda.

73
00:06:43.089 --> 00:06:47.900
Use the same-medium speed to explain the wavelength change.

74
00:06:47.900 --> 00:06:54.433
Start by checking that both tones travel in the same air.

75
00:06:54.433 --> 00:07:00.965
Then compare the frequencies and answer the pitch question immediately.

76
00:07:00.965 --> 00:07:07.498
After that, use wave speed equals frequency times wavelength to calculate or compare the wavelengths.

77
00:07:07.498 --> 00:07:14.031
Finish by stating the misconception shield: the higher-pitch tone is the higher-frequency tone, and a louder sound would not automatically count as a higher-pitch sound.

78
00:07:14.031 --> 00:07:20.564
That is the cleanest route into the worked example.

79
00:07:20.564 --> 00:07:27.096
Keep this takeaway in view: Lesson 3 becomes much safer once the learner can keep pitch, wavelength, and loudness from blending together.

80
00:07:27.096 --> 00:07:33.629
Do not skip the medium check: It tells you whether a wavelength comparison can be made using one shared sound speed.

81
00:07:33.629 --> 00:07:40.162
Do not skip the direct pitch claim: State which tone has the higher frequency and therefore the higher pitch before calculating anything else.

82
00:07:40.162 --> 00:07:46.695
same medium -> treat speed as medium-owned.

83
00:07:46.695 --> 00:07:53.227
compare frequency -> answer the pitch question.

84
00:07:53.227 --> 00:07:59.760
The best answers keep loudness language separate from pitch language all the way through.
